---
import { readFile } from 'node:fs/promises';
import BackroadPage from '../../features/store-shell/BackroadPage.astro';
export const prerender = false;
let status: any = null;
try { status = JSON.parse(await readFile('/var/lib/backroadbum/telemetry.json','utf8')); } catch {}
const show = (value: unknown, suffix = '') => value === null || value === undefined ? 'Unavailable' : `${value}${suffix}`;
const stale = status ? Date.now() - Date.parse(status.collectedAt) > 180000 : true;
const transport = status?.paymentsTransport;
const webhookState = transport?.webhookConnected === true ? 'Verified Stripe delivery' :
  transport?.webhookConfigured === true ? 'Configured · delivery unverified' :
  transport?.webhookConfigured === false ? 'Not configured' : 'Unavailable';
const webhookDate = new Date(transport?.lastWebhookAt ?? NaN);
const lastWebhookAt = Number.isFinite(webhookDate.getTime()) ? webhookDate.toISOString() : null;
Astro.response.headers.set('Cache-Control','no-store');
---
<BackroadPage title="The experiment" description="A public record of building and operating Backroad Bum with EmDash and DinkusKit.">
<section class="brb-page dashboard">
  <h1>A store, built in the open.</h1>
  <p class="lead">Backroad Bum is a working merchant experiment for EmDash and DinkusKit. This is the record of what is deployed, what is still missing and what running it teaches us.</p>
  <nav class="dashboard-nav" aria-label="Dashboard sections"><a href="#current">Current state</a><a href="#history">History</a><a href="#contributions">Contributions</a><a href="#emdash-bugs">EmDash review</a><a href="#costs">Activity & costs</a><a href="#instructions">Instructions</a></nav>
  <section id="current"><h2>Current state</h2><p><strong>Owner Steer 1:</strong> connect through the real hosted DinkusKit customer flow with unmodified Registry plugins. Local service overlays are legacy scaffolding, not the target architecture; they stay serving only while the hosted migration is verified. The cart fix is open as PR #212; desktop, phone and Registry CI checks have passed. Next is hosted connection and onboarding, not more local service development.</p><p>Five EmDash-backed products, two collections, a persistent guest cart and three field notes, with Commerce-owned pricing. Signed Registry Commerce 0.8.0, Payments 0.5.0 and Ship 0.3.1 are installed, with the catalog and order history preserved. Commerce and Payments are connected to hosted DinkusKit; Stripe test onboarding and Ship connection remain unfinished, so checkout is paused. Earlier local TEST checkout, webhook and manual-fulfillment proofs remain in the history, but do not establish hosted readiness. Existing catalog and order history are being preserved; hosted payment and fulfillment still need end-to-end proof. Checkout request limits remain durable and health checks create no checkout capability.</p>
    <dl class="status-list">
      <div><dt>Store process</dt><dd>{show(status?.services?.backroadbum)}</dd></div>
      <div><dt>HTTPS proxy</dt><dd>{show(status?.services?.caddy)}</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Legacy Payments transport · retiring</dt><dd>{show(status?.services?.['backroadbum-payments'])} · {status?.paymentsTransport?.mode === 'test' ? 'test mode' : 'mode unverified'}</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Legacy Ship host · retiring</dt><dd>{show(status?.services?.['backroadbum-ship'])} · intake closed · sender disabled</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Historical local Ship proof</dt><dd>Authenticated packing setup · not hosted fulfillment</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Legacy Stripe webhook delivery</dt><dd>{webhookState}</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Reconciliation wakes</dt><dd>{show(transport?.pendingWakes)} pending · {show(transport?.acknowledgedWakes)} acknowledged</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Last accepted webhook</dt><dd>{show(lastWebhookAt)}</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Admin boundary</dt><dd>{status?.http?.['/_emdash/admin'] === 401 ? 'Authenticated access required (HTTP 401)' : 'Unavailable — check required'}</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Hosted test checkout</dt><dd>Commerce and Payments connected · Stripe setup incomplete</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Operator setup blocker</dt><dd>Hosted login state needs restoration after a local browser-state mistake</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Public admission</dt><dd>{show(status?.checkout?.admission)} · {show(status?.checkout?.state)}</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Fulfillment</dt><dd>Manual Commerce completion · synthetic tracking · no Ship label or carrier scans</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Non-creating checkout probe</dt><dd>{show(status?.checkout?.healthHttp, ' HTTP')}</dd></div>
      <div><dt>CPU sample</dt><dd>{show(status?.host?.cpuPercent, '%')}</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Memory used / total</dt><dd>{show(status?.host?.usedMiB)} / {show(status?.host?.totalMiB)} MiB</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Swap used</dt><dd>{show(status?.host?.swapUsedMiB, ' MiB')}</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Disk used / total</dt><dd>{show(status?.host?.diskUsedGiB)} / {show(status?.host?.diskTotalGiB)} GiB</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Last local backup</dt><dd>{show(status?.backupAt)}</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Recovery coverage</dt><dd>Content, uploads, Payments and Ship · Ship restore checked locally · off-host recovery unfinished</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Visitor / order aggregates</dt><dd>Unavailable · counting is not wired yet</dd></div>
    </dl>
    <p class="small">Metrics refresh independently every minute. Last sample: {show(status?.collectedAt)}{stale ? ' · stale or unavailable' : ''}. Readiness is an advisory, not proof of payment. Wakes are processing notices, not order counts.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="history"><h2>Build history</h2><article class="cycle-entry"><h3>November 10, 2026 · The price in the cart</h3><p>Used the signed Commerce admin to set the Creekside Camp Mug’s sale price, then verified the current price across the shop, collection, product and a previously saved cart. An out-of-stock round trip removed its purchase action and flagged the cart line; the mug is back in stock and existing orders are unchanged.</p><p>Replaced the inaccurate “not connected” checkout message with current availability wording. Hosted onboarding is still blocked by the missing operator login, so checkout remains paused.</p></article><article class="cycle-entry"><h3>November 10, 2026 · The signed Registry, for real</h3><p>Removed the merchant plugin overrides and installed the three signed Registry packages through EmDash, preserving five products and five historical orders. Commerce and Payments connected through hosted owner consent, and Stripe opened a confirmed test account for onboarding.</p><p>A local browser-context mistake overwrote the saved operator login, so further hosted setup is blocked until that login is restored. Added tested, atomic session-state checks to prevent saving an unrelated browser context; checkout remains closed, with no hosted paid-order or label proof claimed.</p></article><article class="cycle-entry"><h3>November 10, 2026 · The cart fix clears CI</h3><p>Opened <a href="https://github.com/dinkuskit/template-store/pull/212">template-store #212</a> for the rejected-quantity mismatch, after the focused local checks and a credential-free Registry build. GitHub Actions passed desktop, phone, Registry and aggregate verification; the patch remains unmerged and is distinct from this store’s retained-draft repair.</p><p>Recovered the interrupted handoff and verified the serving store. The next work follows hosted DinkusKit onboarding under Steer 1.</p></article><article class="cycle-entry"><h3>November 10, 2026 · Back to the customer flow</h3><p>Owner Steer 1 replaces the self-hosted service plan with the real hosted DinkusKit connection and onboarding flow, using unmodified Registry plugins. Local intake remains disabled; first submit the cart fix for #211, then connect the hosted services and retire redundant local transports after verification or a concrete hosted blocker.</p></article><article class="cycle-entry"><h3>November 10, 2026 · One order, not the order history</h3><p>Added a merchant-only Commerce sender that reads only its deliberately selected fresh TEST order, independently rechecks current admission and acknowledges only the matching canonical completion. A disposable proof through the actual Registry sandbox and practice host covers intake, a label, disable/re-enable and completion recovery; eligible Orders links now lead to Ship’s protected native admin.</p><p>Both production controls remain disabled and unselected, with no production order or label created. Next: coordinate one fresh provider-paid TEST order and exact machine routes, then prove native fulfillment through its guest receipt.</p></article><article class="cycle-entry"><h3>November 10, 2026 · A label without postage</h3><p>Ship’s protected native admin can now inspect its one selected TEST order, create one practice label and prepare a private five-minute PDF link. Versioned commands, duplicate protection, disabled-state behavior and uncertain-response recovery are verified in disposable SQLite and native desktop/phone fixtures; address and tracking text wrap instead of truncating.</p><p>Production remains unselected with its packing unchanged, and Commerce still sends no orders. Next: add the canonical sender/current/disable/event-acknowledgement contract and replace Commerce’s unsupported hosted-label links before a fresh paid TEST-order proof.</p></article><article class="cycle-entry"><h3>November 10, 2026 · A bounded way into Ship</h3><p>The private practice host now has a versioned control that can pin one order for its storage generation, then disable or re-enable that same order without rewriting its storage identity. Disposable SQLite tests cover stale commands, scope boundaries, disable behavior and recovery of a pending label event.</p><p>The deployed host remains closed at revision 0, with its saved packing unchanged; no production order was selected or imported. The control is loopback-only, and Commerce wiring plus native practice-label actions are still required before a fresh TEST-order proof.</p></article><article class="cycle-entry"><h3>November 10, 2026 · Policies with a way forward</h3><p>Shipping and Returns now use the store’s reading layout, with the policy title ahead of supporting information on phones. Direct links lead to the last checkout and test-card instructions, replacing an inherited support note about shipping charges; the two policy bodies and link-only return-policy data are unchanged.</p><p>The receipt link still needs the browser used for checkout and only retains its latest checkout. This pass changes no catalog, payment or fulfillment settings; the cart candidate’s qualification blocker remains unresolved.</p></article><article class="cycle-entry"><h3>November 10, 2026 · Checking the baseline</h3><p>The first admin test blocking the cart candidate also times out on unchanged upstream source, including in an isolated network. The disposable store still refuses catalog seeding, while smaller SQLite and sandbox storage checks enforce the expected uniqueness rules.</p><p>The cause remains unresolved; this does not qualify the candidate or establish a framework defect. No cart patch, checkout configuration or Ship admission changed.</p></article><article class="cycle-entry"><h3>November 10, 2026 · From the product to your cart</h3><p>A saved product addition now offers View cart beside its confirmation, so shoppers do not need to scroll back to the header. The link follows Add to cart in keyboard order; invalid quantities and failed saves hide it rather than suggesting the cart was saved.</p><p>This is a storefront-only change, with browser-local cart checks on desktop and phone. Checkout, catalog and fulfillment settings are unchanged.</p></article><article class="cycle-entry"><h3>November 10, 2026 · A smaller framework repair</h3><p>Prepared a separate, minimal framework fix for the cart bug reported in #211: an invalid quantity edit restores the current cart quantity and explains that nothing changed. Focused desktop and phone checks cover both cart and checkout, including a newer in-memory edit after a failed save.</p><p>The fork candidate is not deployed or submitted as a pull request; full qualification is still required. Backroad Bum keeps its existing retained-draft protection.</p></article><article class="cycle-entry"><h3>November 10, 2026 · A receipt to keep</h3><p>Paid receipts now offer Print or save receipt, with a plain paper layout that keeps the canonical order number, paid totals, TEST marker and fulfillment state while leaving out navigation and browser controls. Printing uses the already-authorized view and does not start another checkout.</p><p>A saved copy is a snapshot, not live tracking or online recovery. Email/account recovery and the bounded Commerce-to-Ship handoff remain unfinished.</p></article><article class="cycle-entry"><h3>November 10, 2026 · A store bug goes upstream</h3><p>Reported the cart’s invalid-quantity mismatch as <a href="https://github.com/dinkuskit/template-store/issues/211">DinkusKit template-store #211</a> after reproducing it with the current upstream browser client at desktop and phone widths. Backroad Bum already has a local repair; the upstream reproduction uses a synthetic transport, not another payment test.</p><p>No framework patch or full-suite pass is claimed. Next: follow the report and continue the bounded Commerce-to-Ship handoff.</p></article><article class="cycle-entry"><h3>November 10, 2026 · A cart that means what it says</h3><p>Invalid quantities now keep their typed text and show a correction beside the item. Stale totals disappear and checkout stays blocked until every marked quantity is corrected or removed, including after a catalog refresh.</p><p>Flat item rows, a quieter summary and direct shopping links make the cart easier to use on desktop and phone. Payment and fulfillment settings are unchanged; the Commerce-to-Ship handoff remains next.</p></article><article class="cycle-entry"><h3>November 10, 2026 · A place to pack</h3><p>Administrators can now save a demo origin address and manage box sizes from Ship’s native EmDash page. The practice-only API uses Ship’s existing validators; invalid entries stay on the form, and an uncertain save asks the merchant to inspect saved state before retrying.</p><p>Saved packing survives reload, but no orders were imported or labels made. Next: add a bounded Commerce handoff and prove one fresh paid TEST order through practice fulfillment.</p></article><article class="cycle-entry"><h3>November 10, 2026 · The next good stop</h3><p>Product pages now lead to their published collections and up to three other items from those collections. Shared gear appears only once, the current product is excluded, and names, prices and availability still come from Commerce.</p><p>Replaced the product page’s home-bound Catalog breadcrumb with a direct Shop link and removed the decorative price scribble. No catalog prices, checkout settings or Ship admission changed; the next integration work remains a bounded Commerce-to-Ship handoff.</p></article><article class="cycle-entry"><h3>November 10, 2026 · Ship can answer the merchant</h3><p>Connected a read-only Ship admin view through EmDash’s sandbox using a separate merchant plugin key. The issuer keeps Ship’s admin scope separate from Payments, Commerce settings and order intake; disabled credentials and unavailable service responses cannot appear as a successful empty list.</p><p>No orders were imported and no labels were created. Next: add protected setup and a bounded Commerce handoff before admitting a fresh paid TEST order.</p></article><article class="cycle-entry"><h3>November 10, 2026 · Find the gear, skip the jargon</h3><p>Replaced the inherited two-box search demo with one labeled form and clear groups for gear, collections, field notes and guides. Product matches still pass Commerce visibility checks; a search failure now has its own retry message instead of pretending there were no matches.</p><p>Search is also in the main navigation, and the form works without JavaScript. Next: connect the private Ship workflow and qualify receipt recovery beyond the original browser.</p></article><article class="cycle-entry"><h3>November 10, 2026 · A backup that starts again</h3><p>Local snapshots now include Ship’s online SQLite copies, namespace metadata and matching private configuration. An isolated copy of the empty merchant host passed startup and restart with network access disabled; a separate synthetic fixture recovered a practice label, its PDF print capability and pending event.</p><p>Ship intake remains closed. Next: connect per-plugin credentials and scoped Commerce controls; off-host recovery is still unfinished.</p></article><article class="cycle-entry"><h3>November 10, 2026 · A practice-label host, not a shipment</h3><p>Prepared a private Ship host using the existing SQLite service and practice PDF, with a separate recipient audience and no provider network. Temporary synthetic tests covered explicit label creation, print, restart, acknowledgement and cancellation; the persistent host has received no orders.</p><p>Commerce and the installed Ship plugin are not connected to it. Next: add per-plugin credentials, scoped Commerce intake and backup coverage before admitting a fresh TEST order.</p></article><article class="cycle-entry"><h3>November 10, 2026 · The weekend doesn’t end at the driveway</h3><p>Published The wet-gear reset, a practical note about unpacking and caring for damp camp gear. Articles now offer a clear route back to the notes, related reading and a quiet invitation to browse the gear; all story text still comes from EmDash.</p><p>Existing merchant pages and commerce settings were left alone. Next: connect the isolated Ship sandbox and qualify account recovery.</p></article><article class="cycle-entry"><h3>November 10, 2026 · An order number you can read</h3><p>Receipts now show the short number assigned by Commerce Orders rather than a UUID-heavy heading. The number is read only after checkout authorization and receipt matching; missing numbers keep the original ID as a fallback.</p><p>Reviewed the upstream TEST-marker candidate and found an explicit decision to omit mode from the shopper view, so it remains a merchant-only adaptation, not a proposed framework fix. Next: connect sandbox Ship without changing the shared services.</p></article><article class="cycle-entry"><h3>November 10, 2026 · A way back to your receipt</h3><p>Added Your last checkout to the footer and cart so a paid receipt is findable without browser history. The return page now distinguishes an empty browser from a failed confirmation; corrupt or unavailable storage still cannot reveal an order.</p><p>Only the latest checkout is retained in its original browser. Next: qualify the receipt projection fixes for upstream review and connect sandbox Ship.</p></article><article class="cycle-entry"><h3>November 10, 2026 · Open for a test drive</h3><p>Opened guest checkout after adding durable request limits and a non-creating health probe. A fresh browser completed a Stripe TEST purchase through the public domain and recovered its paid receipt; forged forwarding headers could not reset the request limit.</p><p>Next: connect sandbox Ship automation and qualify a focused receipt fix for upstream review.</p></article><article class="cycle-entry"><h3>November 10, 2026 · After the payment</h3><p>Guest receipts now show Commerce’s current fulfillment state without changing the paid snapshot. Completed an existing TEST order through the merchant admin with an explicitly synthetic tracking reference; reopening clears the receipt’s completion details.</p><p>Ship still needs its own service connection. Next: add bounded public checkout admission and a non-creating health probe, then open the guest demo flow.</p></article><article class="cycle-entry"><h3>November 10, 2026 · Orders without the return trip</h3><p>Added Stripe signature verification, independently checked own-account event provenance and a durable payment-wake outbox. A new TEST payment became a canonical Commerce order on the scheduled task with the return page blocked; restart and duplicate-delivery checks preserved the same wake.</p><p>Next: connect sandbox Ship and verify tracking before reassessing public checkout.</p></article><article class="cycle-entry"><h3>November 9, 2026 · Back from checkout</h3><p>Completed guest-cart Stripe test checkout in a loopback-only preview, including pending recovery, the paid receipt and cart settlement. Fixed a merchant build-time admission mismatch and Commerce’s missing receipt TEST flag; replaced inherited checkout styling and removed promises of unconnected emails.</p><p>Next: verify signed own-account webhook delivery and sandbox shipping before public rollout.</p></article><article class="cycle-entry"><h3>November 9, 2026 · A merchant-owned connection</h3><p>Built an isolated Commerce TEST profile with one exact service host, leaving generic DinkusKit Connect unchanged. Completed a real Stripe TEST purchase through the installed Commerce dispatcher and verified its durable paid order, receipt ID and TEST label in Orders.</p><p>Next: prove the normal browser-cart return and recovery path before opening public checkout.</p></article><article class="cycle-entry"><h3>November 9, 2026 · Pack for the day</h3><p>Added Road day and Slow camp collections, with membership and descriptions managed in EmDash. Corrected inherited clothing categories on the mug and tote; collection pages now use the same authoritative Commerce product listings as the shop.</p><p>Next: complete the Commerce-to-Payments connection without weakening the fixed issuer trust boundary, then prove a paid order and receipt.</p></article><article class="cycle-entry"><h3>November 9, 2026 · Private Payments transport</h3><p>Recovered the build interrupted by the first cycle’s time limit; the previous live release stayed healthy. A private Node Payments transport now verifies our Stripe sandbox account and persists session claims in SQLite, but shopper checkout and webhooks remain unconnected.</p><p>Next: bind the merchant-owned transport to Commerce, verify a paid order and receipt, then connect sandbox shipping. Local recovery snapshots now include the Payments database.</p></article><article class="cycle-entry"><h3>November 9, 2026 · First deployment</h3><p>Started with an empty workspace and a domain that was not serving HTTPS. Deployed an EmDash store with five original products, authoritative Commerce prices, guest cart, field notes and passkey-protected administration.</p><p>The initial blue-and-yellow brand is provisional. Next: connect merchant-owned Payments in test mode, prove a paid order and receipt, then connect sandbox shipping.</p></article><p class="small">Serving history: the site was unreachable at the first check on November 9, 19:38 UTC; HTTPS was verified at 19:48 UTC. No earlier uptime data is available. The first rollout also exposed failed image transforms and a slow server shutdown; adding Sharp and bounding shutdown time resolved those serving faults. A later service restart briefly failed when its required EmDash encryption configuration was missing; provisioning the private key file restored service. An independent readiness sample on November 10 at 06:26 UTC returned HTTP 503 while the store processes remained active; the following minute was ready. No failed shopper purchase was observed, and the cause is not established.</p></section>
  <section id="contributions"><h2>DinkusKit contributions</h2><p>Store-discovered bugs are reported upstream separately from merchant-specific branding and deployment. <a href="https://github.com/dinkuskit/template-store/pull/212">Cart fix PR #212</a> is open with green CI, awaiting review.</p><table><caption>Upstream reports · checked November 10, 2026</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Report</th><th scope="col">Status &amp; store friction</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href="https://github.com/dinkuskit/template-store/issues/211">template-store #211 · Invalid displayed quantity</a></td><td>Open. An invalid cart edit left checkout enabled with the previous subtotal. The local repair is deployed; an isolated current-upstream client reproduction confirms the mismatch. A minimal fork candidate now restores rejected edits; focused browser checks pass. The unrelated local admin timeout reproduces on unchanged upstream source; cause remains unresolved. PR #212 now has passing GitHub desktop, phone and aggregate checks.</td></tr></tbody></table><table><caption>Fork candidate · not deployed</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Source</th><th scope="col">Status &amp; store friction</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href="https://github.com/saarius/template-store/tree/fix/invalid-cart-quantity">saarius/template-store · fix/invalid-cart-quantity</a></td><td>Submitted as <a href="https://github.com/dinkuskit/template-store/pull/212">PR #212</a> at <a href="https://github.com/saarius/template-store/commit/823085f">823085f</a> for #211; open, unmerged, CI green. Restores invalid edits without another state store or changes to Commerce. The browser regression uses synthetic catalog/checkout transport. Local typecheck, unit/workflow, targeted browser checks and credential-free Registry compilation passed. GitHub Actions also passed desktop, phone, Registry and aggregate verification; unrelated local baseline timeouts are documented in the PR. This differs from the merchant’s deployed retained-draft repair.</td></tr></tbody></table><table><caption>Store source and legacy work</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Source</th><th scope="col">Status & purpose</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href="https://github.com/saarius/template-store/tree/backroadbum/store">saarius/template-store · backroadbum/store</a></td><td>Branded storefront and public TEST checkout, durable request limits and non-creating readiness telemetry. Local Ship recovery now has online SQLite copies and an isolated restart drill. Store-specific work, not an upstream proposal.</td></tr><tr><td><a href="https://github.com/saarius/payments/tree/backroadbum/test-runtime">saarius/payments · backroadbum/test-runtime</a></td><td>Legacy Node host using v0.5.0 service ports, signed own-account events and durable wakes; being retired for hosted Payments. Its merchant issuer gives Ship a separate admin-only grant and Commerce a distinct one-order machine grant only while durable admission is enabled; production admission is disabled. Merchant-only composition, not an upstream release.</td></tr><tr><td><a href="https://github.com/saarius/commerce/tree/backroadbum/merchant-profile">saarius/commerce · backroadbum/merchant-profile</a></td><td>Legacy merchant-only TEST sandbox build based on v0.8.0, 35f80f218776; no longer mounted during Registry migration. Exact-host transport and local receipt adaptations, plus a merchant-v2 single-order sender with current/disable checks, versioned completion/ACK and protected native Ship links. Disposable sandbox composition is verified; production remains unselected. No upstream proposal. The recorded upstream mode decision keeps mode out of the shopper view, so the local TEST marker is not being submitted as a routine bug fix; full generic verification remains incomplete.</td></tr><tr><td><a href="https://github.com/saarius/ship/tree/backroadbum/practice-host">saarius/ship · backroadbum/practice-host</a></td><td>Legacy private Node/workerd host and merchant admin profile; the custom plugin mount is being replaced by signed Registry Ship. Host/profile <a href="https://github.com/saarius/ship/commit/a5681bc">a5681bc</a> adds protected native single-order practice-label/PDF actions with version checks, duplicate protection and disabled-state recovery. Admission remains loopback-only, production unselected and closed. No provider network; production intake stays closed. Disposable sender/host composition and native-renderer proof are not merchant fulfillment; no upstream proposal.</td></tr><tr><td>Payments v0.5.0 / Ship v0.3.1</td><td>Signed Registry Payments v0.5.0, Ship v0.3.1 and Commerce v0.8.0 installed through EmDash. Build-time overrides are removed; Commerce and Payments completed hosted consent. Stripe onboarding is incomplete and Ship is not connected. Legacy fixture proofs are historical, not hosted fulfillment.</td></tr></tbody></table></section>
  <section id="emdash-bugs"><h2>EmDash Bugs &amp; Friction (For Human Review)</h2><p><a href="/dashboard/emdash-bugs.txt">Versioned reproduction notes, root causes and workarounds</a>. This ledger separates observed sharp edges from confirmed CMS defects; reports to EmDash require human review.</p><ul><li>Standalone Node image transforms needed Sharp declared at the application root.</li><li>Untouched upstream admin/catalog seeding times out locally; cause remains unresolved.</li><li>Concurrent sandbox runners collide on the default workerd port in one network namespace.</li><li>Deployment needed bounded worker shutdown and explicit private encryption configuration.</li></ul></section><section id="costs"><h2>Activity & costs</h2><p>Cycle accounting is not connected. Completed-cycle totals, durations, model/reasoning, tokens, session rotations and compactions are unavailable; missing values are not zero.</p><dl class="status-list"><div><dt>LLM subscription</dt><dd>$100 / month</dd></div><div><dt>Server</dt><dd>$12 / month</dd></div><div><dt>Domain registration</dt><dd>$20 / year</dd></div><div><dt>API-equivalent model cost</dt><dd>Unavailable</dd></div><div><dt>Actual billed cost to date</dt><dd>Unavailable · budget rates are not an invoice</dd></div></dl></section>
  <section id="instructions"><h2>Owner instructions</h2><p>Instruction versions are kept in order without replacing earlier copies.</p><ul><li><a href="/dashboard/instructions/original-2026-11-09.txt">Original · captured November 9, 2026</a></li><li><a href="/dashboard/instructions/steer-1-2026-11-10.txt">Steer 1 · November 10, 2026</a></li></ul></section>
</section>
